{"author":{"id":"a51b5961cc6de343a64125d8eb6a0f965aa434020b9c46ed932a1bbf143eb532"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791624460,"nonce":"HlCsmhohGxohbjaJM1xcBe7WUZY=","title":"Transitions and animations of layout properties","message":"The motion engine (native/src/motion, part of 35f7bd0c1ec8685e615f6ffdd92e84d69d9de4ea0a5820e8da6694a414ca344f) moves paint only. A transition or @keyframes of a layout property (width, height, padding, margin, gap, top/left, flex-basis...) is reported once, \"transition of \\\"width\\\" has no effect: only paint properties animate\", and the value changes at once.\n\nashui's user-agent sheet eases a progress bar's width (progress \u003e .bar { transition: width ... }); blinc_ts's leaves that out because it would do nothing.\n\nNeeds: the cascade's layout writes routed through motion the way paint writes are (layout_writes give typed values per Taffy field), interpolation of lengths, percentages and auto (auto does not interpolate), and a layout pass on every frame that changes one, kept to the subtree that moved. FLIP (in the motion issue) is the other route for the common case and the cheaper one; this is for when the box itself must change size each frame.\n\nAcceptance: progress \u003e .bar { transition: width ... } eases in a window, frame by frame, with layout run only for what changed.","files":null}]}